y  about everything you're on and what you  really need to be on  and finally i'm a cardiologist so i have  to put in a plug for cardiovascular  disease and knowing your numbers and  risk factors it's still the leading  cause  of death of both men and women in this  country well we mentioned resolutions at  the top and i think this is related to  heart health  weight loss that is a very common  resolution  and people have had some struggles in  2020 and myself included because gyms  were closed in many cases for at least  portions of the year  so what do people do in 2021 to get back  on the treadmill so to speak  it's so true and almost every single one  of my patients has told me that they've  really  taken a nose dive when it comes to their  nutrition diet and exercise but just  because gyms are closed doesn't mean  it's an excuse not to be active you can  certainly get outside  whether it's for a run a walk a bike  also you can do  sports that maybe don't require close  contact things like tennis  there's so many fitness apps you can  stream classes  youtube has dance classes you can do so  there are definitely ways to stay active  in your home and you want to make it if  you can if you have kids too a family  activity a lot of our kids are in front  of screens  all day you want to make sure that  they're getting the recommended 60  minutes of exercise aerobic exercise  daily  and for adults for cardiovascular health  it's about 150 minutes of aerobic  exercise per week that you want to focus  on that's a good reminder i'm not sure i  was getting that but i was greatly  relieved that i was able to put on this  tux with only limited strain  before this conversation so good on me  one final question i checked the tape  and last year  your new year's resolution was increased  mindfulness  one year later how did you do  we did pretty good we i say because it  became a whole family  resolution i downloaded an app called  calm which i  love and i now recommend to all 